upport is crucial for managing the care of these patients effectively. [Data: Entities (15611, 15500); Relationships (20182)]", "# Refeeding Syndrome and Nutritional Management\n\nThe community focuses on the critical medical condition known as Refeeding Syndrome (Síndrome de Realimentación), which poses significant health risks to malnourished patients during nutritional rehabilitation. Key entities include Refeeding Syndrome, Hydroelectrolytic Imbalances (Alteraciones Hidroelectrolíticas), and patients with Anorexia Nervosa (Pacientes con AN), all of which are interconnected through their implications for patient care and management.\n\n## Refeeding Syndrome as a critical health risk\n\nRefeeding Syndrome is a serious clinical condition that can arise in patients who have experienced severe malnutrition and are subsequently reintroduced to nutritional support. This syndrome is characterized by a range of metabolic complications that can occur when nutrients, particularly carbohydrates, are administered too quickly. It poses significant health risks, including severe electrolyte imbalances, arrhythmias, and even coma, making careful monitoring and management essential. Patients at risk for this condition include those who are malnourished, especially after periods of fasting or following surgical procedures. The reintroduction of feeding must be approached with caution, as the sudden influx of calories can lead to dangerous metabolic disturbances. [Data: Entities (641); Relationships (3464, 4441, 919, 4443, 4445)]\n\n## Hydroelectrolytic Imbalances as a related concern\n\nHydroelectrolytic imbalances refer to disturbances in the levels of electrolytes in the body, which can lead to serious health issues, particularly in malnourished patients. These imbalances often necessitate careful monitoring and management to prevent complications and ensure the well-being of affected individuals. The relationship between Refeeding Syndrome and hydroelectrolytic imbalances is critical, as the refeeding process can exacerbate these disturbances, highlighting the need for vigilant oversight during nutritional rehabilitation. [Data: Entities (2075); Relationships (3086, 4443)]\n\n## Vulnerability of Anorexia Nervosa patients\n\nPatients with Anorexia Nervosa (AN) are particularly vulnerable to developing Refeeding Syndrome during their treatment. This population often experiences severe malnutrition, making them at high risk for complications when nutritional support is reintroduced. The management of these patients requires specialized care to prevent the onset of Refeeding Syndrome, emphasizing the importance of tailored nutritional strategies and close monitoring. [Data: Entities (8942); Relationships (4445)]\n\n## Importance of careful nutritional management\n\nThe management of nutritional support, whether through parenteral or enteral nutrition, is crucial in preventing Refeeding Syndrome. Improper administration of nutrition can lead to rapid metabolic changes that trigger this syndrome, underscoring the need for healthcare providers to implement careful protocols during the refeeding process. This includes monitoring electrolyte levels and adjusting nutritional intake based on individual patient needs. [Data: Relationships (4441, 2756, 4440)]\n\n## Thiamine deficiency as a critical factor\n\nRefeeding Syndrome is associated with thiamine deficiency, indicating a critical relationship that must be addressed during nutritional rehabilitation. Thiamine plays a vital role in carbohydrate metabolism, and its deficiency can lead to severe complications when feeding is resumed. Therefore, ensuring adequate thiamine levels in patients at risk is essential for preventing the onset of Refeeding Syndrome. [Data: Relationships (4444)]", "# Nutritional Management in Acute Renal Failure\n\nThis community focuses on the critical interrelationships between various nutritional conditions and interventions in patients with acute renal failure. Key ent