rglycemia\n\nInsulin resistance is a condition that complicates the management of hyperglycemia, particularly in patients with chronic kidney disease and those undergoing surgical procedures. The failure of the body's cells to respond effectively to insulin can exacerbate hyperglycemia, making it crucial to address insulin sensitivity in treatment plans. This relationship highlights the need for integrated approaches in managing patients with both conditions. [Data: Entities (10088); Relationships (8821)]\n\n## Impact of parenteral nutrition on hyperglycemia\n\nParenteral nutrition is closely related to hyperglycemia, as improper management can lead to elevated blood glucose levels. This is particularly concerning for diabetic patients and those receiving intensive nutritional therapy. Careful monitoring and adjustment of nutritional strategies are essential to mitigate the risk of hyperglycemia in individuals undergoing parenteral nutrition, emphasizing the importance of tailored nutritional care. [Data: Entities (2073); Relationships (4663)]\n\n## Refeeding syndrome and its complications\n\nThe syndrome of refeeding occurs in severely malnourished patients undergoing intensive nutritional replenishment, leading to various metabolic complications, including hyperglycemia. This condition underscores the importance of cautious nutritional management in at-risk populations to prevent adverse metabolic responses during refeeding. Understanding this relationship is vital for healthcare providers managing malnourished patients. [Data: Entities (2071); Relationships (8794)]\n\n## Pancreatitis as a contributor to hyperglycemia\n\nAcute pancreatitis is associated with hyperglycemia, complicating the treatment and management of affected patients. Elevated blood glucose levels can hinder recovery and require careful monitoring and intervention. This relationship highlights the need for healthcare providers to be vigilant in managing blood glucose levels in patients with pancr